The village elders commanded Selene to weave away all their misfortunes—droughts, poverty, and sorrow. Day and night she worked, capturing every hardship in thread and watching them fade from existence.

Years passed, and the village became a paradise without suffering. But the people grew strange. They no longer helped one another, for there was no need. They forgot how to comfort friends or celebrate small joys. Their songs became hollow, their stories empty.

One wise child asked Selene, "Why do we feel so lost in this perfect place?"

Selene understood. She had woven away struggle, but also purpose and growth. That night, she destroyed her loom and burned her tapestries.

As flames consumed the threads, the hardships returned—but so did compassion, resilience, and meaning. The people learned that life's beauty exists not despite challenges, but because of them.

Selene never wove again, teaching that some things should not be erased, for they make us whole.
